The Japanese {\TeX} ecosystem has developed largely on its own terms, and much
of it remains opaque outside Japan: the engines are widely used
domestically but rarely elsewhere, and English documentation is scarce.

This
article offers a snapshot of that ecosystem in 2026 for an international
audience. It reviews what makes Japanese typesetting structurally different
from Western typesetting, describes the two workflows in everyday use\Dash
{\pTeX} family and {\LuaTeX}-ja\Dash and the different philosophies behind them,
and examines the challenges the community now faces: accumulated technical debt,
the pace of {\LaTeX} kernel evolution, severely limited maintainer capacity,
and the still-tentative np{\TeX} concept. It is offered from the perspective of
an observer of both the local Japanese and the global {\TeX} communities, and
is based on a talk given at TUG~2026.
